    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green (3/3 passing, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe endpoint is an OfficeFloor function pipeline, so I mirrored the existing `EnsureUniqueOwnerTelephone` guard pattern:\n\n1. **`EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold`** (new function class) \u2014 runs after telephone-uniqueness and before `BuildOwner`. It reads the validated `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` body and compares `lastName` + `address` against every existing owner, both normalized (trim \u2192 collapse internal whitespace \u2192 lower-case via `Locale.ROOT`). A match throws a checked `DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException`. If the request set `sharesHousehold: true`, it returns early and skips the check.\n\n2. **`DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException`** (checked exception) + escalation YAML + **`DuplicateOwnerHouseholdExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT`, matching how the telephone-duplicate rule is handled.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureUniqueHousehold` step into the pipeline under the same `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `sharesHousehold` boolean to the `OwnerFields` schema (`writeOnly`, request-only override), so the generated `OwnerFieldsDto` carries the flag for the guard to read. Without it, Jackson 3 (which ignores unknown properties by default) would silently drop the flag.\n\nThe three acceptance tests cover exactly the spec: same lastName+address \u2192 409; same lastName but different address \u2192 2xx; same lastName+address with `sharesHousehold: true` \u2192 2xx.",
